#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088803} :activity/id #uuid "9b243943-aace-5eea-bc2e-df71d7e7d8c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805 :topic/id #uuid "6834e4fa-5e01-5d57-8ef2-14960e40b519" :topic/math-academy-id 882} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088947 :tutorial/content "Recall that a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **exponential forcing** if $f(x)$ is an exponential function. For example, the ODE below has exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nThe method for solving a second-order ODE with exponential forcing is similar to the method for solving a second-order ODE with polynomial forcing. The general solution is still the sum of the complementary solution $y_c$ (i.e., the solution to the associated homogeneous ODE) and a particular solution $y_p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny = y_c + y_p\n$$\n\nThe only difference is in how we find a particular solution. Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ provided that $e^{bx}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{bx}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a known constant and $\\alpha$ is an unknown constant to be determined." :tutorial/id #uuid "a08b4b47-ae27-534c-b39b-1696d513b79e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4063 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "02ab851a-75bb-55bf-ac1f-e123bca5af20" :step/math-academy-id 593872 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088954 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following second-order ODE with exponential forcing:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =10 e ^ {2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -3$ or $\\lambda = -2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $10e^{2x},$ and $e^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2 \\alpha e ^{2x},\n\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 4  \\alpha e ^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})+6\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ 20\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{2x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bf4c356b-2a4a-5811-905f-b7e539bdc8d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4138 :tutorial/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "1cb38143-b564-54a8-bcbd-0ba84a764e50" :step/math-academy-id 593873 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087869 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087603 :question/id #uuid "b3b024c9-282e-55c3-9300-0db6b88cd3c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-6609"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03260b0d-f1c4-5c40-89dc-3de2d1017148" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404551}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "90d04c15-aa41-5e9b-9985-a256cbc67b1f" :step/math-academy-id 593874 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088948 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ *is* a term in the complementary solution, then $\\alpha e^{bx}$ *cannot* be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{bx}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3 e ^ {-2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +3) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = -2$ or $\\lambda = - 3.$ Consequently, the complimentary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution to the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $3e^{-2x},$ and $e^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x}, \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}-4\\alpha {e}^{-2x}+5({-2\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―}+\\alpha {e}^{-2x})+{6\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}}_{―} & =3{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =3{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = 3 x e^{-2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}+3x{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "76ee6f3c-a454-54c3-8075-56c1c0a27261" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4064 :tutorial/title "The Case When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "236677d6-a196-55c3-9d1f-b2c7cf414ef7" :step/math-academy-id 593877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087604 :question/id #uuid "0d5c1b8c-6f6e-53d6-b2de-034a3f193fef" :question/math-academy-id "e-6610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61611329-9ecd-5917-a11e-3b5effa69673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088451} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404558}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "08b8d2e2-bf6e-5a6b-97ae-b1e8fa3a2ee4" :step/math-academy-id 593878 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088808}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088949 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59c57ff3-a1a4-5ed4-be1c-15350d769274" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4065 :tutorial/title "Second-Order ODEs With Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"} :step/id #uuid "8641b402-b33c-58ce-8d54-a569e2caa272" :step/math-academy-id 593881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087605 :question/id #uuid "d655af30-3da8-50cb-8c06-c3860bf88b5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c01e7705-e8a6-5712-ad9a-2fc77ee3cdec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432978}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE with Exponential Forcing and Repeated Roots"} :step/id #uuid "309ded05-b534-5412-a7ef-d8e18e600691" :step/math-academy-id 593882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087614 :question/id #uuid "bbf37829-4b55-5a39-bf20-d067b34db8fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-6787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "749a2a49-4181-5be7-b5ef-3e5f8d82219f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432977}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"} :step/id #uuid "7099023d-1af5-5693-a8f7-552a9070f7be" :step/math-academy-id 593885}] :activity/title "Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087947}]}
